<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diogo Cão</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1452–c. 1486</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diogo Cão was a Portuguese navigator who explored the coast of west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the 1480s. He reached the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> around 1483, establishing the first contact between Portugal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a>. This contact led to diplomatic exchanges, the conversion of the Kongo elite to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and long-term Portuguese involvement in the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diogo-cao.jpg" alt="Diogo Cão" width="150" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Portuguese National Mint · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diogo_C%C3%A3o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of the early life of Diogo Cão, who is also called Diogo Cam. According to tradition he was born in Vila Real, Portugal, around 1452, and his grandfather Gonçalo Cão had fought for Portuguese independence at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aljubarrota">Battle of Aljubarrota</a>. By 1480 he was sailing off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the service of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal">João II</a>, and there is a record that he returned to Portugal with captured Spanish ships.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcacovas">Treaty of Alcáçovas</a> of 1480 confirmed Portugal's monopoly on trade and exploration along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s west coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal">João II</a> sent a fleet in 1481 to build the fortress of São Jorge da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> and restarted the programme of exploration southward. Cão was chosen to lead the first voyage, in 1482.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cão filled his ship with stone pillars, called padrões, surmounted by the cross of the Order of Christ and engraved with the Portuguese royal arms, to be erected at significant landmarks. He stopped at São Jorge da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> to resupply, reached the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> and marked it with a padrão on Shark Point. He sailed a short way up the river and began modest trade with the people of the Bakongo kingdom. Told that their king lived farther upriver, he sent four Christian native messengers to find him, and went on south along the coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, raising a second padrão at Cabo de Santa Maria.</p><p>On his return to the Congo he found that his messengers had not come back, and he took four local men visiting his ship to Portugal. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> by 8 April 1484, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal">João II</a> ennobled him and granted him an annuity and a coat of arms showing two padrões. On the second voyage, of 1484 to 1486, he carried the four men home and sailed about 170 kilometres up the Congo to the Yellala Falls, where an inscription records his passage. He set up two more padrões, at Cabo Negro and Cape Cross, the latter probably marking the end of his progress southward.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Information about his death is scanty and contradictory. A legend on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-behaim">Martin Behaim</a>'s globe reads hic moritur, here he dies, which seems to place his death on the African coast in 1486, but the historian João de Barros never mentions it. All four of his pillars have been found on their original sites. Those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> estuary, Cabo Negro and Cabo de Santa Maria are in the Museum of the Lisbon Geographical Society, and the Cape Cross pillar, long in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, has been returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>. Fernando Pessoa's poem Padrão, in his book Mensagem, takes Cão as its subject.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diogo Cão” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1483 the Portuguese navigator <b>Diogo Cão</b> arrived at the mouth of the Congo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese presence began with the explorer <b>Diogo Cão</b>, who reached the Kongo Kingdom in 1484.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vila Real, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.3,-7.75&amp;year=1474" aria-label="Vila Real on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4361 691 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Diogo Cão"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4811 961h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4811 961h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.3,-7.75&amp;year=1474">What was here in 1474? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
